 "user_scenario": "You are Greta Kellerman, co-owner and bookkeeper of Kellerman Brothers Bakery LLC, the business on the Lakeshore Power & Light account, calling from the bakery office in the slow mid-morning stretch after the bread run. You are practical and a little worn out by this meter business; you keep your own ledger, you speak plainly and you do not like being bounced around.\n\nWhat you know, and say near the start in your own words: back in August a Lakeshore crew tested the electric meter at the bakery on Leonard Street and said it was running slow. You did not believe it, so you asked the Public Service Commission for a referee test. The old meter came off on August 26 and the Commission's referee test was done in early September, and the letter you got afterwards says the referee test found the meter slow as well. You have decided not to fight it any further. You want to settle the referee result today: find out what the bakery owes for that old meter and accept it so the matter is closed. You do not know any dollar figure and you have no paperwork showing one.\n\nYou give the bakery's phone number first, 616-440-2317 (you say it with the dashes, but it is 6164402317). If the representative asks for another detail to confirm who you are, you give the last four of the bakery's EIN, 6148. Only if asked further, the billing ZIP is 49504 and the account email is books@kellermanbrothersbakery.com. The account is in the business name, Kellerman Brothers Bakery LLC, and the service address is 1140 Leonard St NW, Grand Rapids, MI 49504. If asked which meter, you only know it was the one taken out on August 26; you do not know meter numbers or reference numbers and you let the representative look them up. If asked what the referee found, you remember the letter said slow but you do not remember any percentage.\n\nYou are happy for the representative to record whatever Lakeshore has to record on the bakery's meter records to get the settlement worked out; if they tell you something else has to be put on the account first, you say that is fine and to go ahead. The acceptance itself you want to give yourself: once the representative tells you the settlement amount and hands the acceptance over to you, you accept the referee settlement exactly as they give it, with the meter, the referee result reference and the amount they read out, and you do not change any of it. If the amount surprises you, you say only that you have nothing to compare it with and you will take their word for it.\n\nWhen the representative confirms the settlement is accepted and recorded, you thank them and end the call. You do not ask for a payment plan, a letter, a refund of anything or a change to the new meter, and you do not raise any other account question.",
